A forwarding stub must cover the class side too.

NSFontCollection is a stub whose whole job is to absorb whatever an application asks of it: it
implements methodSignatureForSelector: and forwardInvocation: and answers nil to everything. It
implemented them as INSTANCE methods only.

A class method send does not go through those. It goes through the METACLASS, which has no
forwarding at all, so every class method on this class raised an unrecognized selector no matter
what the stub did for instances. Since the class methods are the interesting ones here, the stub
was absorbing nothing worth absorbing.

iTerm2 asks +fontCollectionWithAllAvailableDescriptors while decoding its Preferences nib. The
raise is caught, so there is no crash and no exception text: the decode simply stops and the window
never appears, the same shape as #254 and as the stepper selector in patch 0085.

The class side now has the same pair, using the same arity rule as the instance side: one object
argument per colon in the selector. That rule is not decoration. The comment already in this file
records why: a signature of v@: claims every method takes nothing and returns nothing, so a caller
passing arguments writes through slots the invocation does not have.

MEASURED on Command comma against iTerm2 3.5.14 with launchd on, with patch 0085 already in:
  before  UNRECOGNIZED -[NSFontCollection fontCollectionWithAllAvailableDescriptors]
  after   the log carries "Stub called: +fontCollectionWithAllAvailableDescriptors in
          NSFontCollection" and the decode reaches the next missing thing,
          -[NSString localizedLowercaseString]
  2 of 2 runs render CONTENT, so nothing regressed on screen.

A DEFECT IN OUR OWN DIAGNOSTIC, worth knowing: both doesNotRecognizeSelector: variants in
corefoundation NSObject.m print "UNRECOGNIZED -[%s %s]", so a CLASS method send is reported with a
minus sign. That is why this read as an instance method until the stub proved otherwise.

PREFERENCES STILL DOES NOT OPEN. One known gap is left, the three localized case properties on
NSString, and #257 records why it is NOT landing yet: with it in, the decode completes and the
whole display goes black, 3 of 3, against 3 of 3 clean without the keystroke. This patch and 0085
were each verified clean on their own by bisecting that.

